Mesh: bound the chunk element counts, which size a resize before anything is read.

Every chunk in `Mesh::load` opens with a `uint32` element count taken straight
off the file and validated nowhere:

	this->materials.resize(c->read_uint());
	this->vertices.resize(c->read_uint());
	this->features.resize(c->read_uint());
	auto poly_count = c->read_uint();
	this->geometry.resize(poly_count);

Same shape as the OUTDOORS counts patch 0034 bounds, and the same reason none of
them can fail on its own: `proto::read_chunked` hands the callback the *whole*
reader (`include/zenkit/Stream.hh`), and a read past the end of the archive
returns nothing rather than throwing. So the loop that follows a corrupted count
neither throws nor stops early -- it runs to the count's own value over reads
that return zero bytes, and the `resize` before it has already committed the
memory the file asked for.

Measured on the unpatched reader against `zenkit-node`'s 1.4 KB fixture world,
with a single count rewritten in place to 0x0FFFFFFF (268 million):

	VERTICES   3.2 GB committed, "LOADED" after 1.6 s
	FEATURES   8.6 GB committed, "LOADED" after 5.1 s
	POLYGONS   "LOADED" after 13.9 s

Not a crash and not a hang -- a world that reports success having taken every
byte of memory the machine could give it, which in the editor is a
`worker_threads` worker inside the user's session. An *absurd* count is the
harmless case, exactly as in 0034: 0xFFFFFFFF vertices are 51 GB, `resize`
throws `bad_alloc` and the load fails loudly.

The fix bounds each count by the bytes actually left in the reader, using the
same `_bytes_remaining` helper as `world/BspTree.cc` for the same reason -- a
vertex is 12 bytes on the wire, a feature 24, a polygon 24 before any index
follows (25 on G1, so 24 refuses nothing valid), a shared lightmap and its
texture 40 each, a non-shared lightmap 76, and an archived material at least a
byte. It cannot change what a valid world parses: a real world carries the data
its counts declare.

The remaining unvalidated-file-data sites in `docs/plans/level-editor.md` 16.11
are the VOB readers, and the `ReadMemory::seek` decision that would close the
class in one place instead of one call site at a time.

Upstreamable as-is: a plain robustness bug, no API change, seven guards.

